Wine (CrossOver 26.3) 32-bit DirectSound FIR resampler SSE2/SIMD optimization.
Built into kit/payload/i386-windows/dsound.dll with:
  i386_CFLAGS="-O2 -msse2 -mfpmath=sse -mstackrealign"
(Wine's i386_EXTRACFLAGS already passes -mpreferred-stack-boundary=2; verified
0 memory-operand movaps/movapd, identical PE exports/imports, "Wine builtin DLL"
stub preserved).

Session 12 x87sidecar profiling showed Wine's stock i386 dsound.dll FIR
resampler (x87 float math) accounted for ~32.5% of all x87 ARM emissions in
32-bit Call of Duty titles. Replacing x87 with SSE2 scalar/4-wide SIMD dot
product, removing double-precision tap promotion, and fast-pathing 16-bit PCM
unpack (avoiding per-sample i386 x87 st(0) float return ABI):
  - Headless 24-voice FIR benchmark (11-44.1 -> 48 kHz):
    100.4% CPU (saturated) -> 14.1% CPU (7.15x faster, -86% CPU; 18.2% even
    without x87sidecar vs 100.3% with 92% audio dropouts).
  - Numerical accuracy across all 12 rate pairs (11025-96000 -> 44100/48000):
    max error <= 7.15e-7 (-123 to -129 dBFS), <= 1 LSB in 16-bit PCM.

